00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.960
Jamison LoCascio, filmmaker: Hi guys want to thank Film Courage for
doing this interview with us today.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.650
My name is Jamison LoCascio.  I'm a
feature film, director, writer and

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.880
producer. Hi my name is Adam Ambrosio. I'm a
producer in one of the writers of SUNSET.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.960
myself and my producing partner 
Adam Ambrosio have just completed our second

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.460
feature film both of our feature films
have distribution have name actors have

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.630
been in festivals and have won awards
we've also produced seven SAG short

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.359
films that have been all around the
country and have distribution with

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:32.950
DirecTV and AT&amp;T says a little
background on us now we wanted to come

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.760
on film courage today first of all film
courage is an excellent channel it's a

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.850
great website and everything else if
you're an indie filmmaker out there and

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.950
you're just joining them for the first
time you should definitely check out

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.030
film courage these guys are great people
and they just want to help independent

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:49.600
filmmakers like us the thing that film
courage asked us to do today was to come

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.930
on and talk about six things we learned
while making our first feature film the

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:57.900
depths that we applied to our second
feature film sunset

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.609
definitely one of the first things I
would say is to trust your passion and

00:01:01.609 --> 00:01:05.180
to choose your advisors carefully the
people that come on and help you with

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.450
the film you have to trust these people
they'll give you a lot of information

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.729
and it's it is definitely hard to find
people that know where you're at in the

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:17.210
space that you're in and have the right
information for you so a lot of times as

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:22.280
the filmmaker comes down to you to find
the research to work very hard putting

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.320
it together
so you have to sort of trust your

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.460
instincts but you have to sort of build
those instincts first and build that

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:34.159
knowledge yourself and you yourself may
you may be your own best advisor rather

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:37.159
than someone out there who's not
familiar with what you're capable of or

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:42.460
what you can do maybe they don't know
what you have access to and they maybe

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:47.270
will misguide you based on the
information that they do know and that

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:50.740
they that they don't know etc

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:55.340
if you have the option to do it on a
smaller crew I would say that's probably

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:01.060
the best form to do it especially if the
budget slow you you just want a couple

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.470
of good you know dedicated people who
you know you could work with and that's

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:12.020
all you need for sunset it was me and
Jamison and two other people on a

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.959
four-man crew and we did a full feature
and sometimes that might be the best way

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:23.709
depending on you know what the project
demands

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.110
know what you absolutely need to make a
film and not just something that looks

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:31.590
lit see or glamorous this can apply to
the camera that you're using when you're

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.819
making a film it can apply to an actor
that you're casting a lot of times it'll

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:39.780
seem like you're adding this this
incredible value to your movie without

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.680
you realizing that you really only need
this little thing a lot of a perfect

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.610
sample of that maybe is that we used the
we used the Blackmagic and we didn't

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:51.900
shoot in 4k on our first feature film we
did shooting 4k on Sunset actually on a

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.650
much lesser budget but that's an
important thing that we that we felt

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.530
sort of defined who we were and what we
were doing at the time with our first

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.170
feature film the depths even though we
had named actors we had other things

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.290
going on in there we didn't feel it was
important to shoot in 4k and we were

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.160
right about that ultimately now on
Sunset we did do it we had the ability

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.019
to do it and the situation presented
itself to us with her with our team so

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:17.580
we did do it that second time and it was
nice but we definitely realized that it

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.750
wasn't that critical at the time to be
that glitzy or that glamorous we just

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.780
wanted to make our movie tell our story
and sometimes you have to realize what

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.590
is best for your film and not
necessarily what other filmmakers are

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.190
doing with their movies that they may
find important may not be important at

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.579
all for what you're doing and the realm
that you're in there's a lot of it's

00:03:35.579 --> 00:03:38.850
understandable because there's a lot of
bad information out there too so you

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.810
have to sort of go we went to the IFP
film lab we studied a lot of different

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:47.790
courses and film distribution and we
figured out how to do PR ourself so a

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.850
lot of that research that we learned on
our own wound up being the most valuable

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.680
stuff that we could have learned out
there

00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:00.890
you'd like to use sag actors we find
that the saddle solo agreement is very

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.019
doable and very fair for what we need

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.959
definitely another important thing too
is when you do we actually went out we

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:13.560
won an award best feature film at
Manhattan Film Festival we started

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.169
winning some acting awards Michael
Rispoli and patch Dara are both

00:04:16.169 --> 00:04:19.410
incredible actors that are established
in the industry I've had they're all

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:23.370
incredible bodies of work and they were
recognized each individually were

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.110
recognized for their acting work I was
recognized as the director of the Depp's

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:31.620
we've also started getting acting awards
on Sunset and we did win the Best

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.530
Feature I've been having Film Festival
on the depths as soon as we started

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:38.850
getting those awards and those accolades
we we started getting a lot more

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:43.199
attention from distributors and as we
were building up this package we started

00:04:43.199 --> 00:04:46.860
getting offers and things like that the
most important thing I would say is that

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.740
when you finally do start to get those
things you've built up enough

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.520
credibility and street cred for your
movie one of the more important things

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.850
about that is that you you definitely
study those distribution deals and look

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:00.330
at what they're offering you remember
these people that a lot of people don't

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.550
think about it this way they say you
know we're gonna jump off the cliff now

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:06.060
here's the distribution deal we're done
the reality is that whoever you go into

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:10.050
business with as your distributor
becomes your partner and they're there

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.860
they're just as important as the DP you
hire they're just as important as the

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:17.460
producer you're working with they are
your team and part of your team and you

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:20.729
have to work with them so make sure that
those people are you know they're

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:25.830
vetting you so also vet them figure out
how valuable they are and what films

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:28.410
they've done before and everything else
and don't just sign the first

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.560
distribution deal you come to I promise
you that your movie definitely has a

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.130
value over time especially with
streaming and everything else that you

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:39.150
don't just have to cash in on the first
thing that you see oftentimes those

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:43.050
collaborations won't make or break your
movie and it's important not to throw up

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:47.180
your hands and just say we're done we
have distribution now

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:56.300
festivals are not the most important
part of the movie it really is the story

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:01.490
and it's it's the decisions you make it
doesn't necessarily mean that your

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:06.110
movies going to be successful it's a
good way to get promotion out and to get

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:12.230
people interested in and can possibly
invest it into going further with it but

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.810
in terms of if you looking for
distribution

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:18.800
you know it's you're probably not gonna
invite someone or someone's not gonna

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:22.820
show up and say look I just saw the
premiere of it you know I'd like to

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.810
distribute it for you it's not usually
gonna happen that way but we do

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:32.450
recommend doing the festivals because it
because promotion is a big key and it

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.800
also will open a little it could open
some doors you know if you if you play

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.800
your cards right with it hey guys we
want to thank film courage for

